EXT. FISHING TRAWLER "MARGARET ANN" - DECK - NIGHT

A rusted forty-foot trawler wallows in black swell off the breakwater, nets heaped in wet coils, a single work lamp swinging on its cable. Diesel and salt in the air.

SKIPPER BRODIE FENN, 50s, weathered, a gold tooth he shows when he lies, leans against the winch. Beside him stands a STRANGER in a clean dry anorak, far too neat for a boat, holding a tablet in a waterproof sleeve.

STRANGER
You're the captain? I was told the catch would be on ice by midnight.

BRODIE
Catch is in the hold. Ice is a figure of speech.

The STRANGER taps the tablet.

ON TABLET: UNIT 4 - HARBOUR CUSTOMS - INSPECTION 06:00 - CLEAR

STRANGER
Customs clear at six. That gives us six hours to move forty crates of "cod" off this deck and into a van that does not officially exist.

BRODIE
(grinning, tooth flashing)
And who might you be, friend, to know about vans?

The STRANGER glances up, finally, and unzips the anorak. Underneath: a badge, a vest, a holstered sidearm.

MARCUS
Detective Marcus Hale, Maritime Task Force. And I'd stop smiling.

THE CREW (O.S.)
(three voices, shouting, nearly overlapping)
Cap'n! Cap'n, there's lights on the water!

Searchlights sweep across the swell, white and enormous. A bullhorn crackles.

MARCUS
(into his collar)
All units, this is Hale. Boarding in ninety seconds. Nobody leaves this deck.

BRODIE
(very quiet)
You were on my boat for three weeks. You gutted my fish.

MARCUS
(beat)
You make a lovely chowder, Skipper.

The work lamp SWINGS and shatters against the winch. Darkness, then the harsh white of the searchlights on every face.
